    {"id": 12, "type": "manual", "slot": "(b) what one point means",
     "desc": "Part (b): a complete answer connects three things. Substituting the line into the circle gives a quadratic in x; exactly one intersection means that quadratic has a repeated root, so its discriminant is zero; and geometrically that is the line touching the circle at a single point, a tangent. A response that computes the discriminant without saying what a repeated root means for the picture, or that says tangent without the discriminant, has made only one of the two links.",
